Article 41: The arrow directions of direction indicator signal lights pointing left, upward, and right respectively indicate left turn, straight ahead, and right turn.
Article 42: Flashing warning signal lights are continuously flashing yellow lights, reminding vehicles and pedestrians to pay attention and look out when passing, and to pass after confirming safety.
Article 43: At a level crossing where a road intersects with a railway, when two red lights flash alternately or one red light is illuminated, it indicates that vehicles and pedestrians are prohibited from passing; when the red light is extinguished, it indicates that vehicles and pedestrians are allowed to pass.
Section 2: Provisions on Motor Vehicle Passage
Article 44: Where two or more motor vehicle lanes are marked in the same direction on a road, the left side is the fast lane and the right side is the slow lane. Motor vehicles traveling in the fast lane shall travel at the speed prescribed for the fast lane; those that do not reach the prescribed speed for the fast lane shall travel in the slow lane. Motorcycles shall travel in the rightmost lane. Where traffic signs indicate travel speeds, vehicles shall travel at the indicated speeds. Motor vehicles in the slow lane overtaking a vehicle ahead may use the fast lane for travel.
Where two or more motor vehicle lanes are marked in the same direction on a road, motor vehicles changing lanes shall not affect the normal travel of motor vehicles in the relevant lanes.
Article 45: Motor vehicles traveling on roads shall not exceed the speed indicated by speed limit signs and markings. On roads without speed limit signs and markings, motor vehicles shall not exceed the following maximum travel speeds:
(1) On roads without a center line, urban roads: 30 kilometers per hour; highways: 40 kilometers per hour;
(2) On roads with only one motor vehicle lane in the same direction, urban roads: 50 kilometers per hour; highways: 70 kilometers per hour.
Article 46: When a motor vehicle encounters any of the following circumstances while traveling, the maximum travel speed shall not exceed 30 kilometers per hour; among them, tractors, battery-powered vehicles, and wheeled special machinery vehicles shall not exceed 15 kilometers per hour:
(1) Entering or exiting non-motor vehicle lanes, passing through railway crossings, sharp curves, narrow roads, or narrow bridges;
(2) Making U-turns, turning, or going down steep slopes;
(3) Encountering fog, rain, snow, sandstorms, or hail, with visibility within 50 meters;
(4) Traveling on icy, snowy, or muddy roads;
(5) Towing a motor vehicle with a malfunction.
Article 47: When a motor vehicle overtakes, it shall turn on the left turn signal in advance, alternate between high and low beams, or sound the horn. On roads without a center line or with only one motor vehicle lane in the same direction, when the vehicle ahead encounters an overtaking signal from the vehicle behind, if conditions permit, it shall reduce speed and yield to the right. The vehicle behind shall, after confirming sufficient safe distance, overtake from the left side of the vehicle ahead, and after pulling away a necessary safe distance from the overtaken vehicle, turn on the right turn signal and return to the original lane.
Article 48: On roads without central isolation facilities or without center lines, when motor vehicles encounter oncoming traffic from the opposite direction, they shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) Reduce speed and drive on the right, maintaining a necessary safe distance from other vehicles and pedestrians;
(2) On road sections with obstacles, the side without obstacles shall proceed first; however, if the side with obstacles has already entered the obstacle section while the side without obstacles has not yet entered, the side with obstacles shall proceed first;
(3) On narrow slopes, the uphill side shall proceed first; however, if the downhill side has already reached the middle while the uphill side has not yet started uphill, the downhill side shall proceed first;
(4) On narrow mountain roads, the side not adjacent to the mountain body shall proceed first;
(5) At night, when meeting oncoming vehicles, switch to low beams at a distance of more than 150 meters from the oncoming vehicle; when meeting non-motor vehicles on narrow roads or narrow bridges, use low beams.
Article 49: Motor vehicles shall not make U-turns at locations with signs or markings prohibiting U-turns or left turns, as well as at railway crossings, pedestrian crossings, bridges, sharp curves, steep slopes, tunnels, or road sections prone to danger.
Motor vehicles may make U-turns at locations without signs or markings prohibiting U-turns or left turns, but shall not obstruct the passage of other vehicles and pedestrians traveling normally.
Article 50: When a motor vehicle reverses, it shall check the situation behind the vehicle and reverse after confirming safety. Reversing is prohibited at railway crossings, intersections, one-way roads, bridges, sharp curves, steep slopes, or tunnels.
Article 51: Motor vehicles passing through intersections controlled by traffic signal lights shall pass in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) At intersections with guided lanes, enter the guided lane according to the required direction of travel;
(2) Vehicles preparing to enter a roundabout shall yield to motor vehicles already in the intersection;
(3) When turning left, turn on the left side of the center point of the intersection. Turn on the turn signal when turning; at night, turn on low beams when traveling;
(4) When encountering a release signal, pass in sequence;
(5) When encountering a stop signal, stop in sequence outside the stop line. If there is no stop line, stop outside the intersection;
(6) When turning right and encountering a vehicle ahead in the same lane waiting for a release signal, stop and wait in sequence;
(7) At intersections without direction indicator signal lights, turning motor vehicles shall yield to vehicles and pedestrians going straight. For vehicles traveling in opposite directions, right-turning motor vehicles shall yield to left-turning vehicles.
Article 52: Motor vehicles passing through intersections not controlled by traffic signal lights and not directed by traffic police, in addition to complying with the provisions of Article 51, Items (2) and (3), shall also comply with the following provisions:
(1) Where controlled by traffic signs and markings, the party with the right of way shall proceed first;
(2) Where not controlled by traffic signs and markings, stop and look before entering the intersection, and yield to vehicles coming from the right;
(3) Turning motor vehicles shall yield to vehicles going straight;
(4) For vehicles traveling in opposite directions, right-turning motor vehicles shall yield to left-turning vehicles.

Article 53 Where a motor vehicle encounters traffic congestion at an intersection ahead, it shall stop in sequence outside the intersection and wait, and shall not enter the intersection.
Where a motor vehicle encounters a preceding motor vehicle stopped in a queue waiting or moving slowly, it shall queue in sequence, shall not weave through or overtake on either side of the preceding vehicle, and shall not stop and wait within a crosswalk or a grid-line area.
Where a motor vehicle encounters a preceding motor vehicle stopped in a queue waiting or moving slowly at an intersection or road section where lanes are reduced, it shall proceed into the intersection or road section after the lane reduction in sequence, one vehicle per lane alternately.

Article 54 The loading of goods by a motor vehicle shall not exceed the approved load mass stated on the motor vehicle driving license; the loading length and width shall not exceed the body of the vehicle, and the following provisions shall be observed:
(1) For heavy and medium-duty trucks and semi-trailers carrying goods, the height from the ground shall not exceed 4 meters, and vehicles carrying containers shall not exceed 4.2 meters;
(2) For other motor vehicles carrying goods, the height from the ground shall not exceed 2.5 meters;
(3) For motorcycles carrying goods, the height from the ground shall not exceed 1.5 meters, and the length shall not extend beyond the body by more than 0.2 meters. For two-wheeled motorcycles carrying goods, the width on each side shall not extend beyond the handlebars by more than 0.15 meters; for three-wheeled motorcycles carrying goods, the width shall not exceed the width of the body.

Passenger cars, except for luggage racks mounted externally on the body and built-in luggage compartments, shall not carry goods. Where a passenger car carries goods on a luggage rack, the height from the roof shall not exceed 0.5 meters, and the height from the ground shall not exceed 4 meters.

Article 55 Motor vehicles carrying passengers shall observe the following provisions:
(1) Highway passenger buses shall not exceed the approved number of passengers, except for children exempt from tickets in accordance with regulations; where the number of passengers is full, children exempt from tickets in accordance with regulations shall not exceed 10% of the approved passenger capacity;
(2) The cargo compartment of a truck shall not carry passengers. On urban roads, a freight motor vehicle may carry 1 to 5 temporary workers in the cargo compartment provided a safe position is reserved; where the height of the cargo exceeds the side panels of the compartment, no person shall be carried on the cargo;
(3) The rear seat of a motorcycle shall not carry a minor under 12 years of age, and a light motorcycle shall not carry passengers.

Article 56 A motor vehicle towing a trailer shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) Trucks, semi-tractors, and tractors are permitted to tow only one trailer. The lighting signals, braking, coupling, and safety protection devices of the trailer shall comply with national standards;
(2) Small passenger cars are permitted to tow only travel trailers or trailers with a total mass of less than 700 kilograms. The trailer shall not carry passengers;
(3) The load mass of a trailer towed by a truck shall not exceed the load mass of the truck itself.

Large and medium-sized passenger buses, low-speed trucks, three-wheeled automobiles, and other motor vehicles shall not tow trailers.

Article 57 Motor vehicles shall use turn signals in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) When turning left, changing lanes to the left, preparing to overtake, leaving a parking place, or making a U-turn, the left turn signal shall be activated in advance;
(2) When turning right, changing lanes to the right, returning to the original lane after completing an overtake, or parking by the roadside, the right turn signal shall be activated in advance.

Article 58 When a motor vehicle travels at night without streetlights, under poor lighting conditions, or under low-visibility conditions such as fog, rain, snow, sandstorms, or hail, it shall turn on the headlights, clearance lights, and taillights; however, when a following vehicle traveling in the same direction is closely following a preceding vehicle, high beams shall not be used. Motor vehicles traveling in fog shall turn on fog lights and hazard warning flashers.

Article 59 When a motor vehicle passes through sharp curves, slopes, arch bridges, crosswalks, or intersections without traffic signal control at night, it shall alternately use high and low beams to signal.
When a motor vehicle approaches sharp curves, the top of slopes, or other sections affecting safe sight distance, or when overtaking or encountering an emergency, it shall slow down and sound the horn to signal.

Article 60 Where a motor vehicle breaks down or is involved in a traffic accident on a road, obstructing traffic and difficult to move, it shall activate the hazard warning flashers in accordance with regulations and place a warning sign 50 to 100 meters behind the vehicle; at night, the clearance lights and taillights shall also be turned on simultaneously.

Article 61 Towing a faulty motor vehicle shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) The towed motor vehicle shall not carry passengers other than the driver and shall not tow a trailer;
(2) The width of the towed motor vehicle shall not be greater than the width of the towing motor vehicle;
(3) When using a flexible towing device, the distance between the towing vehicle and the towed vehicle shall be greater than 4 meters and less than 10 meters;
(4) For a towed vehicle with brake failure, a rigid towing device shall be used for towing;
(5) Both the towing vehicle and the towed vehicle shall activate the hazard warning flashers.

Crane trucks and wheeled special-purpose machinery vehicles shall not tow vehicles. Motorcycles shall not tow vehicles or be towed by other vehicles.
Faulty motor vehicles with failed steering or lighting/signal devices shall be towed by a special wrecker.

Article 62 Driving a motor vehicle shall not involve the following acts:
(1) Driving when the doors or compartments are not closed properly;
(2) Hanging or placing items that obstruct the driver's vision within the front and rear window areas of the motor vehicle cab;
(3) Making or answering handheld phone calls, watching television, or other acts that obstruct safe driving;
(4) Turning off the engine or coasting in neutral when going down a steep slope;
(5) Throwing or scattering items onto the road;
(6) When driving a motorcycle, removing hands from the handlebars or hanging items on the handlebars;
(7) Continuously driving a motor vehicle for more than 4 hours without stopping to rest, or stopping to rest for less than 20 minutes;
(8) Sounding the horn in areas or road sections where honking is prohibited.

Article 63 Where a motor vehicle temporarily parks on a road, it shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) On road sections with no-stopping signs or markings, within the motor vehicle lane and non-motor vehicle lane, sidewalk, and

sections equipped with isolation facilities, as well as pedestrian crossings and construction sections, parking is prohibited;
(2) at intersections, railway crossings, sharp curves, narrow roads with a width of less than 4 meters, bridges, steep slopes, tunnels, and sections within 50 meters of the aforementioned locations, parking is prohibited;
(3) in front of bus stops, first-aid stations, gas stations, fire hydrants, or fire stations (fire brigades), and sections within 30 meters of the aforementioned locations, except for vehicles using the aforementioned facilities, parking is prohibited;
(4) before the vehicle comes to a complete stop, doors shall not be opened and passengers shall not get on or off; when opening or closing doors, other vehicles and pedestrians shall not be obstructed;
(5) roadside parking shall be close to the right side of the road; the driver of a motor vehicle shall not leave the vehicle, and after passengers get on or off or goods are loaded or unloaded, the vehicle shall depart immediately;
(6) urban public buses shall not stop to pick up or drop off passengers at sections other than designated stops.

Article 64: When a motor vehicle passes through a flooded road or a flooded bridge, it shall stop to check the water conditions, and after confirming safety, pass through at a low speed.

Article 65: A motor vehicle carrying oversized items passing through a railway crossing shall pass through at the railway crossing and time designated by the local railway department.

A motor vehicle passing through a ferry crossing shall obey the commands of the ferry crossing management personnel and wait in turn at the designated location. When getting on or off the ferry boat, the motor vehicle shall proceed slowly at a low speed.

Article 66: When police cars, fire trucks, ambulances, and engineering rescue vehicles encounter traffic obstruction while performing emergency tasks, they may use sirens intermittently and shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) Sirens shall not be used in areas or sections where the use of sirens is prohibited;
(2) Sirens shall not be used in urban areas at night;
(3) When traveling in a column, if the vehicle in front has already used the siren, the vehicle behind shall not use the siren again.

Article 67: Within the premises of a unit or within a residential area, motor vehicles shall travel at a low speed and yield to pedestrians; where there are speed limit signs, they shall travel in accordance with the speed limit signs.

Section 3: Traffic Regulations for Non-Motor Vehicles

Article 68: Non-motor vehicles passing through intersections controlled by traffic signals shall pass in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) Turning non-motor vehicles shall yield to straight-moving vehicles and pedestrians who have the right of way;
(2) When traffic ahead at the intersection is congested, they shall not enter the intersection;
(3) When turning left, they shall turn on the right side of the center point of the intersection;
(4) When encountering a stop signal, they shall stop in turn outside the stop line at the intersection; if there is no stop line, they shall stop outside the intersection;
(5) When turning right and encountering a vehicle ahead in the same direction waiting for the release signal, if they can turn within the current lane, they may proceed; if they cannot turn, they shall wait in turn.

Article 69: Non-motor vehicles passing through intersections not controlled by traffic signals and not directed by traffic police, in addition to complying with the provisions of Items (1), (2), and (3) of Article 68, shall also comply with the following provisions:
(1) Where traffic signs or markings control the intersection, they shall yield to the party with the right of way;
(2) Where there are no traffic signs or markings controlling the intersection, they shall proceed slowly or stop outside the intersection to observe, and yield to vehicles coming from the right;
(3) Non-motor vehicles traveling in opposite directions that are turning right shall yield to vehicles turning left.

Article 70: Persons riding bicycles, electric bicycles, or tricycles crossing motor vehicle lanes on a road section shall dismount and push; where there are pedestrian crossings or pedestrian overpass/underpass facilities, they shall pass through the pedestrian crossing or pedestrian overpass/underpass facilities; where there are no pedestrian crossings, no pedestrian overpass/underpass facilities, or it is inconvenient to use such facilities, they may proceed straight through after confirming safety.

Non-motor vehicles that cannot travel within their own lane due to occupation of the non-motor vehicle lane may borrow an adjacent motor vehicle lane within the obstructed section, and shall quickly return to the non-motor vehicle lane after passing the occupied section. Motor vehicles encountering this situation shall slow down and yield.

Article 71: Non-motor vehicles carrying goods shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) For bicycles, electric bicycles, and motorized wheelchairs for the disabled carrying goods, the height from the ground shall not exceed 1.5 meters; the width shall not extend more than 0.15 meters on each side beyond the handlebars; the length at the front shall not extend beyond the wheels, and at the rear shall not extend more than 0.3 meters beyond the vehicle body;
(2) For tricycles and rickshaws carrying goods, the height from the ground shall not exceed 2 meters; the width shall not extend more than 0.2 meters on each side beyond the vehicle body; the length shall not extend more than 1 meter beyond the vehicle body;
(3) For animal-drawn carts carrying goods, the height from the ground shall not exceed 2.5 meters; the width shall not extend more than 0.2 meters on each side beyond the vehicle body; the length at the front shall not extend beyond the shafts, and at the rear shall not extend more than 1 meter beyond the vehicle body.

Provisions regarding passengers on bicycles shall be formulated by the people's governments of provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government according to local conditions.

Article 72: Persons riding bicycles, tricycles, electric bicycles, or motorized wheelchairs for the disabled on roads shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) Riders of bicycles and tricycles must be at least 12 years old;
(2) Riders of electric bicycles and motorized wheelchairs for the disabled must be at least 16 years old;
(3) Driving under the influence of alcohol is prohibited;
(4) Before turning, they shall slow down and give a hand signal; they shall not make sudden sharp turns; when overtaking a vehicle ahead, they shall not obstruct the vehicle being overtaken;
(5) They shall not tow or hold onto vehicles or be towed by other vehicles; they shall not ride with both hands off the handlebars or hold objects in their hands;
(6) They shall not ride side by side, chase each other, or weave and race;
(7) They shall not ride unicycles or bicycles ridden by two or more persons on the road;
(8) Persons who do not have lower-limb disabilities shall not drive motorized wheelchairs for the disabled;
(9) Power devices shall not be installed on bicycles or tricycles;
(10) Learning to drive non-motor vehicles on the road is prohibited.

Article 73: Persons driving animal-drawn carts on roads must be at least 16 years old and shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) Driving under the influence of alcohol is prohibited;
(2) Driving side by side is prohibited, and the driver shall not leave the vehicle;
(3) When passing through busy sections, inters...

Overtaking is prohibited at intersections, railway crossings, pedestrian crosswalks, sharp curves, narrow roads or narrow bridges with a width of less than 4 meters, steep slopes, tunnels, or road sections prone to danger. When driving a two-wheeled animal-drawn vehicle, the driver shall dismount and lead the animal;
(4) Untamed animals shall not be used to drive a vehicle; young animals accompanying the vehicle shall be tethered;
(5) When parking a vehicle, the vehicle brake shall be tightened and the animal shall be tethered.
Section 4 Traffic Rules for Pedestrians and Passengers
Article 74 Pedestrians shall not engage in the following acts:
(1) Using skateboards, roller skates, or other sliding tools on the road;
(2) Sitting, lying down, staying, or playing in the carriageway;
(3) Chasing vehicles, throwing objects at vehicles, or other acts that obstruct road traffic safety.
Article 75 Pedestrians crossing motor vehicle lanes shall pass through pedestrian crossing facilities; if there are no pedestrian crossing facilities, they shall pass through pedestrian crosswalks; if there are no pedestrian crosswalks, they shall observe the situation of passing vehicles, confirm safety, and cross straight ahead; they shall not suddenly accelerate to cross or retreat or turn back halfway when vehicles are approaching.
Article 76 Pedestrians traveling in formation on the road shall not exceed 2 people in each horizontal row, except that this restriction does not apply to road sections where traffic control has been implemented.
Article 77 Passengers riding in motor vehicles shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) They shall not hail motor vehicles in motor vehicle lanes;
(2) They shall not get on or off motor vehicles from the left side in motor vehicle lanes;
(3) Opening and closing vehicle doors shall not obstruct the passage of other vehicles and pedestrians;
(4) While the motor vehicle is in motion, they shall not interfere with driving, shall not extend any part of their body outside the vehicle, and shall not jump out of the vehicle;
(5) When riding a two-wheeled motorcycle, they shall sit facing forward.
Section 5 Special Provisions for Expressways
Article 78 Expressways shall indicate the driving speed of lanes; the maximum speed shall not exceed 120 kilometers per hour, and the minimum speed shall not be lower than 60 kilometers per hour.
For small passenger cars traveling on expressways, the maximum speed shall not exceed 120 kilometers per hour; other motor vehicles shall not exceed 100 kilometers per hour; motorcycles shall not exceed 80 kilometers per hour.
Where there are 2 lanes in the same direction, the minimum speed of the left lane is 100 kilometers per hour; where there are 3 or more lanes in the same direction, the minimum speed of the leftmost lane is 110 kilometers per hour, and the minimum speed of the middle lane is 90 kilometers per hour. If the speed indicated by road speed limit signs is inconsistent with the provisions on lane driving speeds above, the speed indicated by the road speed limit signs shall be followed.
Article 79 Motor vehicles entering an expressway from a ramp shall turn on the left turn signal and enter the lane without obstructing the normal driving of motor vehicles already on the expressway.
When motor vehicles leave an expressway, they shall turn on the right turn signal, enter the deceleration lane, reduce speed, and then leave.
Article 80 Motor vehicles traveling on expressways shall maintain a distance of more than 100 meters from the vehicle ahead in the same lane when the speed exceeds 100 kilometers per hour; when the speed is lower than 100 kilometers per hour, the distance from the vehicle ahead in the same lane may be appropriately shortened, but the minimum distance shall not be less than 50 meters.
Article 81 Motor vehicles traveling on expressways shall comply with the following provisions when encountering low-visibility meteorological conditions such as fog, rain, snow, sandstorms, or hail:
(1) When visibility is less than 200 meters, turn on fog lights, low-beam headlights, position lights, and front and rear position lights; the speed shall not exceed 60 kilometers per hour, and a distance of more than 100 meters shall be maintained from the vehicle ahead in the same lane;
(2) When visibility is less than 100 meters, turn on fog lights, low-beam headlights, position lights, front and rear position lights, and hazard warning flashers; the speed shall not exceed 40 kilometers per hour, and a distance of more than 50 meters shall be maintained from the vehicle ahead in the same lane;
(3) When visibility is less than 50 meters, turn on fog lights, low-beam headlights, position lights, front and rear position lights, and hazard warning flashers; the speed shall not exceed 20 kilometers per hour, and the vehicle shall leave the expressway as soon as possible from the nearest exit.
When the circumstances in the preceding paragraph occur, the expressway management department shall release prompt information such as speed limits and maintaining vehicle distance through display screens and other means.
Article 82 Motor vehicles traveling on expressways shall not engage in the following acts:
(1) Reversing, driving in the wrong direction, making a U-turn across the central median, or stopping in a lane;
(2) Overtaking on ramps, acceleration lanes, or deceleration lanes;
(3) Riding on or straddling lane dividing lines, or driving on the shoulder;
(4) Driving or parking on the emergency lane in non-emergency situations;
(5) Test driving or learning to drive motor vehicles.
Article 83 The cargo compartment of trucks traveling on expressways shall not carry passengers. Two-wheeled motorcycles traveling on expressways shall not carry passengers.
Article 84 Motor vehicles passing through construction sections shall pay attention to warning signs and drive at reduced speed.
Article 85 The road traffic safety management of urban express roads shall be implemented with reference to the provisions of this Section.
The public security traffic management departments of the people's governments of provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government may designate the public security traffic management departments of the people's governments of cities divided into districts or public security traffic management departments at the equivalent level to undertake the road traffic safety management work of expressways and urban express roads.
Chapter 5 Handling of Traffic Accidents
Article 86 When a traffic accident occurs on a road between motor vehicles or between a motor vehicle and a non-motor vehicle without causing personal injury or death, and the parties have no dispute over the facts and causes, after recording the time and location of the traffic accident, the name and contact information of the other party, the motor vehicle license plate number, driver's license number, insurance certificate number, and collision parts, and signing jointly, they shall leave the scene and negotiate compensation for damages on their own. If the parties have a dispute over the facts and causes of the traffic accident, they shall report to the police promptly.
